    "result_text": "The full build passes (BUILD SUCCESS, all tests green).\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed the locality derivation to prefer the postcode, keeping each piece of logic in its rightful place:\n\n**`RegionPostcodeTable`** (owner of the region\u2194postcode-range mapping) gained a reverse lookup `regionFor(String postcode)`. It returns an `Optional<String>` \u2014 the region whose range contains the postcode, or empty when the postcode is absent, non-numeric, or in no known range. This reuses the existing `RANGE_BY_REGION` table rather than duplicating the ranges.\n\n**`Owner.getLocality()`** now orchestrates the precedence policy in one line \u2014 postcode first, city as fallback:\n```java\nreturn RegionPostcodeTable.regionFor(this.postcode)\n    .orElseGet(() -> CityRegionTable.regionFor(this.city));\n```\n\n`CityRegionTable` and `PostcodeValidator` were untouched \u2014 the city\u2192region table remains the single source of the fallback mapping, and validation is unaffected.\n\nThis satisfies both behaviors the spec/tests require: known cities still resolve to the same region (`knownOwner(\"Sydney\")` with postcode 2000 \u2192 `NSW`), while a VIC-range postcode (3000) on an unknown city now yields `VIC`, proving the postcode takes precedence.",
